# TLS and routing for one host.
#
# A page served over https may only open a wss socket, so every address a player
# dials needs a certificate. Caddy gets one per name automatically over HTTP-01,
# which needs nothing but port 80 reachable and the A records pointing here: no
# DNS credentials, no renewal cron, no certificate in the arena's config.
#
# Routing is by hostname on 443 rather than a port per arena. One port means
# nothing to open in a firewall and nothing for a corporate proxy to object to,
# and it means adding an arena is a name and a compose service rather than a
# port negotiation.
#
# {$VW_DOMAIN} lets the same file serve a real deployment and a local one:
# `VW_DOMAIN=localhost` gives directory.localhost and a1.localhost, which Caddy
# signs with its own internal CA and which browsers resolve to loopback without
# a hosts entry.

# No global block, and specifically no ACME email. Let's Encrypt stopped
# requiring one, the only thing it buys is expiry warnings, and Caddy renews on
# its own -- so the mail would be about a failure mode that is already handled.
# An empty `email` directive is a parse error rather than an omission, which is
# how a blank variable took the whole proxy down in a restart loop.

# Reachable by raw IP over plain http, which is the only thing that works before
# DNS has propagated and a certificate exists. That window is exactly when a
# fresh host is least diagnosable, and it is where a first deploy goes wrong, so
# provisioning writes coarse progress here and Caddy comes up before the build
# rather than after it. Nothing secret is written: anyone can read this.
#
# It shadows the http-to-https redirect Caddy would otherwise generate for the
# named sites below, so http://directory.<domain> answers "vectorwake" instead of
# redirecting. Harmless, because a game client dials wss directly.
http:// {
	handle_path /deploy* {
		root * /srv/deploy
		file_server browse
	}
	handle {
		respond "vectorwake" 200
	}
}

# What a player's client asks for the list of games. Also the socket arena
# servers register over, but they reach it on loopback and never through here.
directory.{$VW_DOMAIN} {
	# Plain HTTP, so the chain up to Caddy can be checked with curl from
	# anywhere. Everything else here is a WebSocket upgrade, which plenty of
	# networks and proxies will not carry, and "cannot connect" is a useless
	# symptom when it could be DNS, the certificate, Caddy, or the game.
	handle /health {
		respond "ok" 200
	}
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:9000
}

a1.{$VW_DOMAIN} {
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:9001
}

a2.{$VW_DOMAIN} {
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:9002
}

# Deliberately absent: the admin surface. docs/architecture/admin.md says a
# secret in a file is not enough to put it on the public internet, and the read
# view needs no token at all, so it would hand a stranger the fleet. It listens
# on loopback and is reached through an ssh tunnel:
#
#   ssh -L 9100:127.0.0.1:9100 root@<host>   then http://127.0.0.1:9100
